# TASK-260715-3ejhyy — developer outcome

## Result

Ready for review. The macOS production root and deterministic SPM harness root now build the same candidate-neutral `TunnelRuntimeFactory` session graph. Production validates the sole accepted M0 manifest before constructing any runtime dependency. `prepare` only creates the packet-plane session; `activateReads` is the sole `PacketFlowBridge.start` call and therefore remains post-settings.

## Accepted binding pin

- Source: `/Users/iv/Developer/relux-tunnel/.temp/resources/TASK-260715-3ejhyy/TASK-260720-1qhxqa_m0-production-bindings-v1.json`
- Size: `17492` bytes
- SHA-256: `40333862b46b7af04cdd966ade91e8c9cc917e573c6601e26e7fa956d11ae161`
- Schema/task/revision semantics, `productionCompositionPermitted`, supersession state, upstream evidence digests, selected HEV/libssh2/bridge pins, compatibility rows, and mandatory capabilities are all fail-closed gates at the production call site `MacOSProductionDependencyFactory.makeRuntime()`.
- Missing and unreadable sources have distinct errors; digest drift is rejected before decoding; semantic negatives cover disabled permission, supersession, pin drift, and missing mandatory capability. No component closure is invoked on rejection.

## Implemented scope

- Added candidate-neutral M1 packet-plane preparation/session/dispatch types in `ReluxTunnelCore`.
- Added macOS production binding validation and one-graph-per-generation composition in `ReluxTunnelMacOSAdapter`.
- Selected the existing libssh2 transport, accepted PacketFlow/HEV path, immutable configuration, mandatory host policy, system-domain Keychain credentials, privacy-safe diagnostics, clocks, metrics, logging, cancellation, memory pressure, settings seams, TCP and DNS consumers through existing boundaries.
- Added deterministic harness session factory with the same root interface and generation semantics, without a production-manifest bypass.
- Enforced TCP CONNECT, virtual-DNS TCP/UDP dispatch, and explicit rejection of all other M1 UDP.
- Existing coordinator and bridge ownership contracts retain reverse-order release and idempotent repeated stop.
- iOS, route/tuning selection, TCP/DNS internals, reconnect, general UDP, and provider implementation changes remain excluded as required.

